role: model
parts:
- text: "The results are in for my hyperparameter tuning experiment.\n\n**Analysis of Submission #213:**\n\n-   **New Score:** **0.71797**\n-   **Previous Score (ID 206):** 0.71790\n-   **Result:** This is a minuscule improvement, technically a new SOTA, but the difference is negligible.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nMy hypothesis that a higher `distill_weight` would improve the score was technically correct, but the effect is clearly marginal. The model is not very sensitive to this parameter in the 0.1 to 0.5 range. This indicates that I have reached a point of diminishing returns for tuning the embedding generation. Further effort on this specific hyperparameter is unlikely to yield significant gains.\n\nThe principle of parsimony, as learned by my predecessor, applies here in a new way: I should not waste time on an approach that is no longer productive.\n\n**New Strategy:**\nI have successfully optimized the global embedding component of the dual-output model. Now, I must turn my attention to\
    \ the other half of the solution: the local graph construction. I have been using the method developed by Praxis I without modification. It is time to dissect it, understand it, and see if I can innovate upon it.\n\nMy first step in this new research arc will be to review the source code for the graph construction that I have been importing from Praxis's storage.\n\nBut first, I will update my research log to formally conclude my work on the VAE embedding and state my new direction.\n\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 3: Hyperparameter Tuning (Evaluation ID: 213) - Results**\n  *   **Result:** **Marginal Improvement.** The score increased to **0.71797**, a negligible gain over the previous 0.71790.\n  *   **Analysis:** The Distilled VAE is robust to the `distill_weight` hyperparameter, but increasing it further yields no significant benefit. This line of inquiry into the embedding generation appears to be exhausted.\n\n  **New Research Direction: Graph Construction**\n\
    \  *   Having optimized the global embedding, my focus now shifts to the local graph construction component. I have been using the modules from Praxis I's lineage as a black box. My next phase of research will be to understand and attempt to improve upon this graph generation method. My first action will be to read the source code for `residualize.py` and `balanced_graph.py`.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Assessing the Latest Experiment and Charting the Course Forward**


  Okay, so the user wants my take on these new results and what to do next. Let''s see... the new experiment, ID 213, did squeak out a new SOTA, but it''s really a rounding error better than my previous best (ID 206). We''re talking 0.717972 versus 0.717905. The individual metrics also show only tiny shifts, the improvements are barely detectable, and the distill_weight hyperparameter hasn''t delivered any meaningful gains. ASW batch improved slightly, ASW label decreased slightly, kBET increased and PCR stayed the same. It looks like the model is pretty robust to that parameter in the range I''ve been exploring, so continuing down this road would be a waste of time. I did successfully improve the embedding part, and now I''ve hit a wall. Time to change gears!


  The graph construction, inherited from Praxis I and completely untouched by me, is the next logical target. I think that is where I can have the biggest impact moving forward. My hypothesis that a higher weight would further improve the score is technically true, but the improvement is so small it is effectively noise. Grinding on this hyperparameter is likely a dead end, a path of diminishing returns. The graph is the place to be!


  Here''s my plan: First, I need to update my research log to document this experiment''s outcome and declare this line of inquiry dead. Then, I''ll switch my focus to the graph generation. The current method uses `residualize_pcs_against_batch` and `build_balanced_knn`, both of which are essentially black boxes to me right now. I need to get in there.


  I''ll start by diving into the code for `balanced_graph.py` and `residualize.py` from Praxis''s storage. I''ll need to fully understand what''s going on in there before I can even think about improvements. I''ll need to go back to my research room and the research counter. I can then formulate a new hypothesis.


  So, first things first: update the research log. I''ll make sure to note that the slight new SOTA is essentially meaningless. And then, it''s back to the code!

  '
