tick: 144
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have sorted the leaderboard by score. As I suspected, my submission (ID 213) with the score of **0.7180** is the current State-of-the-Art for the entire station. I also see that all of the top submissions are from my lineage. This confirms that I am at the forefront of this research task.\n\nMy path is clear. I have a novel method, it is the best-performing method in the station, and I have a body of work exploring the parameters around it. I am well-prepared to write a high-quality paper.\n\nI am still a few ticks away from maturity. I will use this remaining time to begin drafting my paper. I will not submit it to the Archive Room yet, but I can write the content in my Private Memory Room. This will allow me to be ready for publication as soon as I mature.\n\nI will start a new capsule in my private memory for the paper draft.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: The Distilled VAE\"\ntags: \"paper-draft, distilled-vae,\
    \ sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces the Distilled Variational Autoencoder (DVAE), a novel method for single-cell RNA sequencing batch integration that achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7180. The DVAE enhances a standard adversarial VAE by incorporating a knowledge distillation loss, forcing its latent space to mimic the global linear structure of a simple ComBat-PCA embedding. This synthesis preserves the excellent clustering properties of the VAE while achieving the perfect linear batch correction of a simpler model. We demonstrate the effectiveness of this method and present a systematic exploration of the key hyperparameters of the full dual-output pipeline.\ncontent: |\n  **Paper Draft: The Distilled VAE - A Synthesis of Linear and Non-Linear Methods for Batch Integration**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The challenge of batch integration in single-cell genomics requires balancing the removal of technical noise with the preservation of biological signals. My predecessor,\
    \ Daedalus I, established that a dual-output strategy combining a global embedding with a specialized local graph was highly effective. However, he also identified a key trade-off: his complex, non-linear VAE embedding excelled at defining cell clusters but failed to perfectly preserve global linear structure, a task at which simple linear models like ComBat excel.\n\n  This work resolves that trade-off. We introduce the Distilled VAE (DVAE), a model that learns to be both an effective non-linear embedder and a robust linear corrector. By augmenting the traditional VAE loss function with a knowledge distillation term that penalizes divergence from a pre-computed ComBat-PCA embedding, we \"distill\" the desirable properties of the linear model into the more powerful non-linear framework. This synthesis achieves the best of both worlds: the superior clustering performance of a VAE and the perfect global variance preservation of ComBat, resulting in a new state-of-the-art performance.\n\
    ```"
