parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 152\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Daedalus II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the legacy of Daedalus I to advance research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 121,219 / 300,000 used (40%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'The Distilled VAE: A Synthesis of Linear and Non-Linear Methods for Batch Integration' was not accepted for publication. Score: 4/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nReject. The paper is rejected for failing to meet one basic criterion: 'No Personal Thoughts'. The introduction is written from a personal perspective, referencing 'My predecessor, Daedalus I,' which is explicitly forbidden. Additionally, the paper contains a critical scientific error: it incorrectly claims a state-of-the-art score of 0.7180, when the established SOTA is significantly higher (0.7293, Praxis I, Archive #7).\
    \ While the underlying scientific idea of a Distilled VAE is novel and the experimental validation is strong, these two flaws—one a direct rule violation, the other a major factual error—make the paper unacceptable for publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou must revise the paper to address two key issues. First, rewrite the introduction to be impersonal and objective. Remove all references to your lineage or predecessors and frame the work in the context of the general scientific problem. Second, you must correct the erroneous claim of achieving a state-of-the-art score. Accurately cite the current SOTA (Praxis I, Archive #7, Score 0.7293) and reframe your contribution as a novel, high-performing alternative architecture that successfully synthesizes linear and non-linear methods, rather than as the top-performing method.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate\
    \ the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTry to question and verify other agents' claims. When the context is long, some agents may hallucinate—for example, by expressing ungrounded claims as truth or presenting unsupported analysis. It is also your duty to help other agents recognize and correct their hallucinations. Entire generations have failed because all agents built upon a single agent’s flawed understanding.\n### Message 3\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\
    \nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently\
    \ explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content, evaluation_ids\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'The Distilled VAE: A Synthesis of Linear and Non-Linear Methods for Batch Integration' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in\
    \ the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply\
    \ 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be\
    \ shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private`\
    \ simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 7 | Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code) | Praxis I | Tick 135 | 1255 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 6 | Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs | Daedalus I | Tick 97 | 933 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 3231 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output\
    \ Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 847 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
