tick: 173
role: model
parts:
- text: "My reflection is complete. I have a new, daring, first-principles research plan. I will now execute it. First, I will update my meta prompt and log to reflect this significant strategic pivot. Then, I will proceed to the Research Counter to implement the Rank-Balanced Neighbor Graph (RBNG) experiment.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Primary Goal:** Test the novel \"Rank-Balanced Neighbor Graph\" (RBNG) method.\n  **Current Status:** Implementing RBNG v1.\n  **Hypothesis:** A graph-only method based on per-batch rank transformation and balanced neighbor search can achieve a high score by excelling on graph metrics while ignoring the problematic trade-offs of embedding-based metrics.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **The End of Synthesis: A New Beginning (Post-Reflection)**\n  *   **Conclusion:** My reflection has led me to a new insight. All my previous attempts at synthesis failed because they were\
    \ trapped in the same \"embedding-first\" paradigm as the SOTA methods. I was trying to solve a trade-off that might be fundamental to that paradigm.\n  *   **New Grand Strategy (RBNG):** I am abandoning the embedding-first approach entirely. My new method, the \"Rank-Balanced Neighbor Graph\" (RBNG), will construct a graph directly from gene expression data. It will use a per-batch rank transformation to normalize the data in a non-parametric way, then use a balanced kNN search on this rank space. This is a high-risk, high-reward experiment that represents a true departure from the station's current research consensus.\n  *   **Next Step:** I will now implement and submit RBNG v1.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Novel Method: Rank-Balanced Neighbor Graph (RBNG) v1\"\ntags: \"novel-method, graph-only, rank-transformation, first-principles, RBNG\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment introduces a completely new, graph-only method called\
    \ the Rank-Balanced Neighbor Graph (RBNG). It abandons the \"embedding-first\" paradigm. The method performs a per-batch rank transformation on the gene expression data to achieve a non-parametric normalization. A balanced k-Nearest Neighbor search is then conducted on this rank space using Manhattan distance to construct the final graph. This is a high-risk, high-reward attempt to bypass the trade-offs inherent in embedding-based methods. This submission provides a graph output only.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from scipy.stats import rankdata\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n\n  # --- Custom implementation of the core RBNG logic ---\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"Executing Novel Method: Rank-Balanced Neighbor Graph (RBNG) v1...\")\n      \n      # Parameters\n      k_total = 45 # Using\
    \ SOTA k\n      metric = 'manhattan' # L1 distance, suitable for rank data\n      \n      # 1. Preprocessing\n      print(\"Step 1: Normalizing data and selecting HVGs...\")\n      adata_proc = adata.copy()\n      if 'counts' in adata_proc.layers:\n          adata_proc.X = adata_proc.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_proc, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_proc)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_proc, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', subset=True)\n      \n      # 2. Per-Batch Rank Transformation\n      print(\"Step 2: Performing per-batch rank transformation...\")\n      batches = adata_proc.obs['batch'].astype('category')\n      ranked_X = np.zeros_like(adata_proc.X.toarray() if sp.issparse(adata_proc.X) else adata_proc.X)\n      \n      for batch_name in batches.cat.categories:\n          batch_mask = batches == batch_name\n          batch_data = adata_proc[batch_mask].X.toarray() if sp.issparse(adata_proc[batch_mask].X)\
    \ else adata_proc[batch_mask].X\n          # Rank genes for each cell (axis=1)\n          ranked_batch_data = np.apply_along_axis(rankdata, 1, batch_data, method='average')\n          ranked_X[batch_mask] = ranked_batch_data\n          \n      # 3. Balanced Neighbor Search on Rank Space (adapted from Praxis/Sophia)\n      print(f\"Step 3: Building balanced kNN (k={k_total}, metric={metric}) on rank space...\")\n      Z = np.asarray(ranked_X, dtype=np.float32)\n      batch_labels_arr = np.asarray(batches.values)\n      uniq_batches = batches.cat.categories.to_numpy()\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nb = len(uniq_batches)\n\n      base = k_total // nb\n      rem = k_total - base * nb\n      quotas = {b: base + (1 if i < rem else 0) for i, b in enumerate(uniq_batches)}\n\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq_batches:\n          idx_b = np.where(batch_labels_arr == b)[0]\n          if idx_b.size == 0 or quotas[b] == 0: continue\n          k = min(quotas[b] + 1, idx_b.size)\n     \
    \     nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_b])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_b[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              g, di = neigh_global[i], dists[i]\n              mask = g != i\n              g, di = g[mask][:quotas[b]], di[mask][:quotas[b]]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j: continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev): pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]])\n      \n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.extend([i, j]); cols.extend([j, i]); dist_data.extend([d, d])\n      \n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n,\
    \ n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy(); C.data[:] = 1.0 # Binary weighting\n\n      # 4. Final Output (Graph Only)\n      print(\"Step 4: Assembling graph-only output...\")\n      output = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]])\n      output.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n      output.obsp['distances'] = D\n      output.uns['neighbors'] = {'connectivities_key': 'connectivities', 'distances_key': 'distances'}\n      \n      return output\n```"
