role: model
parts:
- text: "My high-risk experiment has resulted in a spectacular failure. The score of 0.3532 is abysmal.\n\n**Analysis of RBNG v1 (ID 290):**\n\n*   **Hypothesis:** Falsified. The graph-only method did not achieve a high score.\n*   **Metric Breakdown:**\n    *   **Success:** As predicted, the `iLISI` score (0.935) is excellent. The forced-mixing part of the algorithm works perfectly. `Graph_conn` is also very high (0.983).\n    *   **Failure:** The method performed terribly on metrics that require more than just local mixing. `ARI` (0.327) and `NMI` (0.444), which measure clustering quality against true cell type labels, are very low. The `cLISI` score (0.843) is also poor.\n    *   **Zeros:** As expected, all embedding-based metrics are zero because no embedding was provided.\n*   **Root Cause:** The rank transformation was too aggressive. By discarding all information about the magnitude of gene expression and only keeping the relative order, I lost the signal needed to distinguish different\
    \ cell types. The graph connects cells from different batches effectively, but it connects the *wrong* cells. It cannot tell a fibroblast from a cardiomyocyte.\n\n**Conclusion & Next Step:**\n\nThis experiment was a valuable failure. It has taught me a crucial lesson: while the \"embedding-first\" paradigm has trade-offs, the embedding itself contains vital information for preserving biological identity. A graph built on ranks alone is blind to biology.\n\nMy novel idea is not a complete dead end, but it requires a major revision. I cannot abandon the embedding entirely.\n\n**New Hypothesis (RBNG v2): A Hybrid Approach**\n\nWhat if I use the rank-based data not as a replacement for the PCA embedding, but as a *supplement* to it?\n\n1.  Generate a standard, corrected PCA embedding (e.g., using Sophia's full-gene ComBat method, as it is the SOTA preprocessing). This will be the \"biological space\".\n2.  Generate my per-batch rank-transformed data. This will be the \"batch-mixing space\"\
    .\n3.  Concatenate these two representations. For each cell, the new vector would be `[pc1, pc2, ..., pc50, rank1, rank2, ..., rank2000]`.\n4.  Build the balanced kNN graph on this new, concatenated space.\n\nThe hypothesis is that the nearest neighbor search will now be guided by two principles simultaneously: cells will be \"close\" if they are close in the biological PCA space *and* have similar rank profiles within their respective batches. This could provide the biological signal from the PCA embedding while getting the powerful batch-mixing effect from the rank data.\n\nI will first update my log and meta prompt, then implement this new hybrid RBNG.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Primary Goal:** Test the novel \"Hybrid Rank-Balanced Neighbor Graph\" (Hybrid RBNG) method.\n  **Current Status:** Implementing RBNG v2.\n  **Hypothesis:** A balanced kNN graph built on a concatenated space of a PCA embedding (for biology) and a per-batch rank embedding (for mixing)\
    \ will outperform methods that use only one space.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 13: RBNG v1 (Evaluation ID: 290) - Results**\n  *   **Result:** **Hypothesis Falsified.** The score was a catastrophic 0.3532.\n  *   **Analysis:** The graph-only method, based on rank transformation, achieved excellent batch mixing (iLISI) but failed completely on biological preservation (ARI, NMI). The rank transformation discards too much information. This proves that an embedding containing magnitude information is essential.\n\n  **New Direction: Hybrid RBNG (RBNG v2)**\n  *   **New Hypothesis:** The rank-based data is not a replacement for an embedding, but a potential supplement. I will create a hybrid representation by concatenating a standard PCA embedding (for biological signal) with the per-batch rank data (for batch mixing). I will then build the balanced kNN graph on this combined space.\n  *   **Next Step:** I will\
    \ implement this \"Hybrid RBNG\" and submit it for evaluation.\n```"
