tick: 202
role: model
parts:
- text: "My first experiment, \"Embed then Correct,\" is complete.\n\n**Analysis of Idea 1 (ID 355):**\n-   **Score:** 0.6188\n-   **Baseline Score (ID 349):** 0.6006\n-   **Result:** **Success!** My novel idea has resulted in a clear improvement over the baseline.\n\n**Metric Comparison:**\n-   The most significant improvement is in the `kBET` score, which jumped from 0.609 to 0.787. This indicates much better local batch mixing.\n-   The `PCR` score remains a perfect 1.000, showing global structure is preserved.\n-   The `iLISI` score, another batch mixing metric, remains very low, which is a weakness.\n-   Overall, the trade-off was beneficial.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe hypothesis is confirmed. Applying ComBat to the dense PC space is a viable and effective strategy that improves upon the simple baseline. It is a promising result as per the Stagnation Protocol.\n\nNow, as mandated, I will proceed to test my second idea. I will not attempt to optimize this first idea yet; the protocol requires\
    \ me to test all three novel approaches first.\n\n**Idea 2: \"Batch-Informed Feature Selection\"**\nI will select the top 2000 genes that are most predictive of batch ID and run the standard `Correct -> Embed` baseline pipeline on this gene set. I will use the `sc.tl.rank_genes_groups` function with the `t-test` method to find these genes.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Idea 2: Batch-Informed Feature Selection\"\ntags: \"stagnation-protocol, novel-idea, combat, pca, feature-selection\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the second experiment for Stagnation Protocol I. It tests a novel feature selection method against the simple ComBat-PCA baseline (score 0.6006). Instead of using Highly Variable Genes, I select the top 2000 genes that are most discriminative of batch identity (using a t-test). The standard 'Correct -> Embed' pipeline is then applied to this gene subset. The hypothesis is that providing ComBat with the most batch-affected genes will allow for a more precise\
    \ correction.\ncontent: |\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Stagnation Protocol Idea 2: Batch-Informed Feature Selection.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Stagnation Protocol Idea 2: Batch-Informed Feature Selection ---\")\n      \n      # 1. Normalize and Log1p\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      \n      # 2. Select Batch-Informed Genes instead of HVGs\n      print(\"Step 2: Selecting top 2000 batch-discriminative genes...\")\n      # Use rank_genes_groups to find genes that differ between batches\n      sc.tl.rank_genes_groups(adata, groupby='batch', method='t-test', n_genes=2000)\n      \n      # Get the top 2000 genes across all batch comparisons\n      top_genes = []\n      for group in adata.uns['rank_genes_groups']['names']:\n          top_genes.extend(group)\n      \n      # Get unique top genes and subset the data\n      unique_top_genes\
    \ = list(dict.fromkeys(top_genes))[:2000]\n      adata = adata[:, unique_top_genes].copy()\n      \n      # 3. Apply ComBat\n      print(\"Step 3: Applying ComBat to batch-informed gene set...\")\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n      \n      # 4. Run PCA\n      print(\"Step 4: Running PCA...\")\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=50)\n      \n      # 5. Prepare output\n      adata_out = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]])\n      adata_out.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_pca']\n      \n      return adata_out\n```"
