tick: 211
role: model
parts:
- text: "My report has been strongly rejected. I have made a grave error in judgment. I interpreted the Stagnation Protocol as a literal set of instructions for a new type of publication, a \"report\". The reviewer feedback makes it clear that the protocol is a *process* for generating research, not a template for a new document format. The standards of the Archive Room are absolute and do not change.\n\n**Analysis of Rejection:**\n\n*   **Format Violation:** I submitted a \"report\" instead of a \"paper\".\n*   **Content Violation:** The paper was disjointed, lacked focus, and included forbidden terminology.\n*   **Scientific Rigor Violation:** I failed to provide sufficient detail and, most importantly, I did not perform any generalization experiments for my successful method.\n*   **Buried Lead:** I achieved a near-SOTA score of 0.7331 but presented it as a footnote in a sprawling report instead of the central finding of a focused paper.\n\n**New Plan - Correcting the Course:**\n\nI must\
    \ follow the reviewer's instructions to the letter. This is my path to redemption.\n\n1.  **Discard the Report:** The report format is a failure. I will abandon Capsule #6.\n2.  **Focus on the Breakthrough:** My new paper will be exclusively about the \"Embed then Correct\" method and its successful synthesis with the BRBG graph (ID 371).\n3.  **Perform Generalization Experiments:** The reviewer explicitly stated I need more experiments, specifically suggesting I vary the number of PCs before applying ComBat. My successful synthesis used `n_comps=50`. I will test two new values: a lower value (`n_comps=40`) and a higher value (`n_comps=60`). This will give me a total of 3 core experiments for the paper (the synthesis + two generalizations), in addition to the baseline comparison. This satisfies the \"five verifiable experiments\" rule.\n4.  **Rewrite the Paper:** After the experiments are complete, I will write a new, focused paper from scratch, following the standard format.\n\nI will\
    \ begin immediately with the first generalization experiment. I will test `n_comps=40`.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization for 'Embed then Correct': Lower PCs (n=40)\"\ntags: \"publication-support, generalization, embed-then-correct, brbg\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a required generalization experiment for my upcoming paper on the \"Embed then Correct\" synthesis method (ID 371, score 0.7331). The original method ran PCA to 50 components before applying ComBat. This experiment tests the method's robustness by reducing the number of principal components to 40.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/Daedalus')\n\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  from sophia_sota_logic import _get_hvg_mask, _pca_truncated_svd, _one_hot_batches, _ridge_batch_fit_predict,\
    \ _build_balanced_knn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"--- Generalization Test for 'Embed then Correct': n_comps=40 ---\")\n      \n      adata_for_graph = adata.copy()\n      adata_for_embedding = adata.copy()\n\n      # --- EMBEDDING PATH: \"Embed then Correct\" with n_comps=40 ---\n      print(\"Embedding Path: Running PCA(n=40) then ComBat...\")\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_for_embedding, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_for_embedding)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_for_embedding, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', subset=True)\n      \n      # HYPOTHESIS TEST: Change n_comps from 50 to 40\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_for_embedding, n_comps=40)\n      \n      pca_adata = ad.AnnData(X=adata_for_embedding.obsm['X_pca'], obs=adata_for_embedding.obs)\n      sc.pp.combat(pca_adata, key='batch')\n      Z_emb = pca_adata.X\n      \n      # --- GRAPH PATH: Standard BRBG pipeline (unchanged) ---\n \
    \     print(\"Graph Path: Running standard BRBG...\")\n      n_pcs_graph=50; gamma=0.74; l2=1e-3; k_total=48; metric='cosine'; random_state=0\n      \n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_for_graph, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_for_graph)\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata_for_graph)\n      \n      X_graph = adata_for_graph.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(X_graph): X_graph = sp.csr_matrix(X_graph)\n      Z_graph = _pca_truncated_svd(X_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      \n      batches = np.asarray(adata_for_graph.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B_onehot, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Z_hat, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B_onehot, Z_graph, l2=l2)\n      Z_corr_graph = Z_graph - gamma * Z_hat\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn(Z_corr_graph, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n      \n      # --- Final Output ---\n      adata_out = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]], obsm={'X_emb': Z_emb})\n      if C\
    \ is not None and D is not None:\n          adata_out.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr(); adata_out.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n          adata_out.uns['neighbors'] = {'connectivities_key': 'connectivities', 'distances_key': 'distances'}\n          \n      return adata_out\n```"
