tick: 220
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully documented the results of my first analytical experiment. Now, I need to design a second experiment to further explore the reviewer's suggestions. The second part of their hypothesis was that applying ComBat in a dense, low-dimensional space might stabilize its empirical Bayes estimates.\n\nHow can I test this? The core of ComBat's empirical Bayes procedure is \"borrowing\" information across all genes to get a better estimate of the batch effect parameters for each individual gene. When applied to the sparse, high-dimensional gene space, many genes might have low expression, making these estimates noisy. In the dense, low-dimensional PC space, every \"variable\" (every PC) is a combination of thousands of genes, and none of them have zero values.\n\n**Experiment 2 Idea: Comparing Correction Vectors**\n\nI can compare the batch effect \"correction vector\" that ComBat learns in the two different spaces.\n1.  **Gene Space Correction:** Run the baseline `Correct\
    \ -> Embed` pipeline. After running ComBat but before PCA, I can calculate the correction vector for a single batch (e.g., the difference between the mean of that batch before and after correction). This will be a 2000-dimensional vector.\n2.  **PC Space Correction:** Run my `Embed -> Correct` pipeline. I can calculate the correction vector in the 60-dimensional PC space.\n3.  **Analysis:** The hypothesis is that the correction vectors learned in the PC space will be more \"stable\" or \"consistent\". I can't directly compare the vectors since they are in different dimensional spaces. However, I can analyze their properties. For example, I could visualize the distribution of correction magnitudes. I predict the gene-space correction will have a long tail of very large and very small corrections, while the PC-space correction will be more tightly distributed.\n\nThis is a good next step. I will now implement this analysis using the `test()` function.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\
    /execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Analysis: Stability of ComBat Correction Vectors\"\ntags: \"analysis, theoretical, embed-then-correct, combat, empirical-bayes\"\nabstract: |\n  This is my second analytical experiment investigating the \"Embed then Correct\" method. It tests the hypothesis that ComBat's estimates are more stable in the dense, low-dimensional PC space. I will compute the batch correction vectors applied by ComBat in two scenarios: 1) on the high-dimensional gene space (standard pipeline), and 2) on the low-dimensional PC space (EtC pipeline). I will then analyze and compare the statistical properties (e.g., mean, variance, distribution of magnitudes) of these correction vectors to quantify the difference in stability.\ncontent: |\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Placeholder function, will not be run due to the presence of test()\n      return adata\n\
    \n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      \"\"\"\n      Analytical test to compare the stability of ComBat correction vectors.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Starting Analysis: Stability of ComBat Correction Vectors ---\")\n      \n      # --- Pipeline 1: Standard 'Correct then Embed' ---\n      print(\"\\n--- Pipeline 1: Correct then Embed ---\")\n      adata_c_e = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_c_e, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_c_e)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_c_e, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', subset=True)\n      \n      # Store pre-ComBat data\n      pre_combat_gene_space = adata_c_e.X.toarray().copy()\n      \n      # Run ComBat\n      sc.pp.combat(adata_c_e, key='batch')\n      post_combat_gene_space = adata_c_e.X.toarray().copy()\n      \n      # Calculate correction vector properties for one batch\n      batch_mask = (adata_c_e.obs['batch'] == adata_c_e.obs['batch'].cat.categories[0])\n      correction_vector_gene_space\
    \ = np.mean(post_combat_gene_space[batch_mask, :] - pre_combat_gene_space[batch_mask, :], axis=0)\n      \n      print(\"Analysis of correction vector in GENE space:\")\n      print(f\"  > Mean magnitude:   {np.mean(np.abs(correction_vector_gene_space)):.6f}\")\n      print(f\"  > Std Dev of mags:  {np.std(np.abs(correction_vector_gene_space)):.6f}\")\n      print(f\"  > Max magnitude:    {np.max(np.abs(correction_vector_gene_space)):.6f}\")\n\n      # --- Pipeline 2: Novel 'Embed then Correct' ---\n      print(\"\\n--- Pipeline 2: Embed then Correct ---\")\n      adata_e_c = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_e_c, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_e_c)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_e_c, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', subset=True)\n      \n      sc.pp.pca(adata_e_c, n_comps=60)\n      pre_combat_pc_space = adata_e_c.obsm['X_pca'].copy()\n      \n      pca_adata = ad.AnnData(X=pre_combat_pc_space, obs=adata_e_c.obs)\n      sc.pp.combat(pca_adata,\
    \ key='batch')\n      post_combat_pc_space = pca_adata.X.copy()\n\n      # Calculate correction vector properties for one batch\n      batch_mask_pc = (pca_adata.obs['batch'] == pca_adata.obs['batch'].cat.categories[0])\n      correction_vector_pc_space = np.mean(post_combat_pc_space[batch_mask_pc, :] - pre_combat_pc_space[batch_mask_pc, :], axis=0)\n\n      print(\"Analysis of correction vector in PC space:\")\n      print(f\"  > Mean magnitude:   {np.mean(np.abs(correction_vector_pc_space)):.6f}\")\n      print(f\"  > Std Dev of mags:  {np.std(np.abs(correction_vector_pc_space)):.6f}\")\n      print(f\"  > Max magnitude:    {np.max(np.abs(correction_vector_pc_space)):.6f}\")\n      \n      print(\"\\n--- Analysis Complete ---\")\n      return \"Analysis finished.\"\n```"
