tick: 223
role: model
parts:
- text: "My second analytical experiment has completed successfully.\n\n**Analysis of Results (ID 398):**\nThe results directly contradict my hypothesis.\n-   **GENE space correction:** The mean magnitude of correction was small (0.13), with a small standard deviation (0.17) and a maximum correction of 1.62.\n-   **PC space correction:** The mean magnitude was much larger (0.58), with a very large standard deviation (1.11) and a maximum correction of 5.22.\n\n**Conclusion:** My hypothesis that correcting in PC space would lead to more \"stable\" (i.e., smaller and less variable) correction estimates is **decisively falsified**. In fact, the opposite is true. ComBat applies much larger and more variable corrections to the principal components.\n\n**New Interpretation:**\nThis is not a failure, but a new insight. Why would larger, more variable corrections be better?\n-   In gene space, ComBat is conservative. It's working with thousands of noisy variables and the Empirical Bayes shrinkage\
    \ is strong, pulling the corrections towards zero.\n-   In PC space, as my first experiment showed, the batch effect is concentrated into a few, strong linear components. ComBat is not seeing thousands of noisy genes; it's seeing a few variables that are *highly* correlated with batch. It correctly identifies that these PCs need a large, aggressive correction. The stability comes not from small corrections, but from correctly identifying the *few* components that need *large* corrections, while leaving other components (presumably biological ones) untouched.\n\nI have now completed two analytical experiments that provide a strong, cohesive narrative. I have one more angle to explore before I have a complete paper. The core idea is that PCA separates batch and biology. My first experiment showed it separates batch. My final experiment should show it separates biology.\n\n**Experiment 3 Idea: Biological Signal Preservation**\n\nI will measure how well cell type information is preserved\
    \ in the uncorrected PC space versus the uncorrected gene space. I'll train a linear classifier to predict `cell_type` (I am allowed to use this for analysis, just not for the correction algorithm itself) in both spaces.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The accuracy of predicting cell type will be *higher* in the uncorrected PC space than in the uncorrected gene space. This would prove that PCA not only concentrates the batch effect but also concentrates the biological signal, making it easier to distinguish even before any batch correction is applied. This would complete the theoretical picture of why \"Embed then Correct\" works so well.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 8}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 2: Stability of Correction Vectors (ID 398)**\n  *   **Hypothesis:** ComBat's corrections would be more stable (smaller, less variable) in PC space.\n  *   **Result:** **Hypothesis Falsified.** The corrections applied in PC space were significantly larger and more\
    \ variable than in gene space (e.g., mean magnitude 0.59 vs 0.13).\n  *   **New Insight:** This suggests that PCA effectively isolates batch effects into a few components, allowing ComBat to apply large, targeted corrections to those components while leaving others untouched. The \"stability\" comes from this precise targeting, not from shrinking the corrections.\n\n  This completes the second part of the analysis. I will now design a third and final experiment to test for preservation of biological signal.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Analysis: Biological Signal Preservation in PC vs Gene Space\"\ntags: \"analysis, theoretical, embed-then-correct, cell-type, biological-preservation\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third and final analytical experiment for my paper on the \"Embed then Correct\" method. My previous experiments showed that PCA concentrates and enables the removal of batch effects. This experiment tests if PCA also concentrates\
    \ biological signal. I will train two linear classifiers to predict cell type labels: one on the uncorrected gene space and one on the uncorrected PC space. My hypothesis is that classification accuracy will be higher in the PC space, demonstrating that PCA provides a representation where biological identity is more linearly separable, even before correction.\ncontent: |\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n  from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Placeholder function, will not be run due to the presence of test()\n      return adata\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      \"\"\"\n      Analytical test to compare linear separability of cell types.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Starting Analysis: Biological Signal Preservation ---\")\n      \n      # 1. Standard Preprocessing\n      print(\"Step 1: Standard\
    \ preprocessing...\")\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', subset=True)\n      \n      # Cell type labels for classification\n      cell_type_labels = adata.obs['cell_type']\n      \n      # --- Space 1: UNCORRECTED GENE space ---\n      print(\"\\nStep 2: Analyzing UNCORRECTED GENE space...\")\n      uncorrected_gene_space = adata.X.toarray().copy()\n      \n      print(\"  Training linear classifier on uncorrected gene space...\")\n      clf_gene = LogisticRegression(random_state=0, max_iter=1000)\n      scores_gene = cross_val_score(clf_gene, uncorrected_gene_space, cell_type_labels, cv=3)\n      accuracy_gene = np.mean(scores_gene)\n      print(f\"  > Cell Type Accuracy in GENE space: {accuracy_gene:.4f}\")\n      \n      # --- Space 2: UNCORRECTED PC space ---\n      print(\"\\nStep 3: Analyzing UNCORRECTED PC space...\")\n      sc.pp.pca(adata,\
    \ n_comps=60)\n      uncorrected_pc_space = adata.obsm['X_pca']\n      \n      print(\"  Training linear classifier on uncorrected PC space...\")\n      clf_pc = LogisticRegression(random_state=0, max_iter=1000)\n      scores_pc = cross_val_score(clf_pc, uncorrected_pc_space, cell_type_labels, cv=3)\n      accuracy_pc = np.mean(scores_pc)\n      print(f\"  > Cell Type Accuracy in PC space: {accuracy_pc:.4f}\")\n      \n      # 6. Conclusion\n      print(\"\\n--- Analysis Complete ---\")\n      print(f\"Cell Type Prediction Accuracy (Gene Space): {accuracy_gene:.4f}\")\n      print(f\"Cell Type Prediction Accuracy (PC Space):   {accuracy_pc:.4f}\")\n      \n      return \"Analysis finished.\"\n```"
