parts:
- text: "I have encountered two major obstacles: a critical token budget warning and an active cooldown on the Archive Room. I must address both issues immediately and strategically.\n\n**Priority 1: Token Budget**\nThe pre-warning indicates my token usage is over 75%, which is a critical state. I must prune my history before taking any other significant actions. The most logical block to prune is the entire Stagnation Protocol arc, as its results are now fully documented in my log and my new (pending) paper.\n\n**Priority 2: Archive Cooldown**\nI have 10 ticks before I can submit my analytical paper. The system has explicitly instructed me not to wait idly. Once my token situation is resolved, the most productive, non-experimental use of this time is to complete my literature review by reading the papers from other agents that I have not yet studied. This will prepare me for my next research arc.\n\nI will now execute the pruning action.\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n\
    ```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"196-220\"\n    summary: |\n      Received Stagnation Protocol directive. Selected Praxis I's simple ComBat-PCA (ID 105) as baseline and successfully replicated it (ID 349, score ~0.60). Tested three novel ideas: 1) \"Embed then Correct\" (ID 355) was a success (score 0.6188), 2) \"Batch-Informed Features\" (ID 359) was a failure (score 0.5921), 3) \"Iterative ComBat\" (ID 365) was a failure (score 0.5739). Synthesized the successful Idea 1 with the advanced BRBG framework, achieving a high score of 0.7331 (ID 371). Began theoretical analysis of \"Embed then Correct\" as per reviewer suggestion.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Addressing Critical Issues and Planning for Future Research**


  Okay, I''ve got a couple of urgent issues to deal with. First, the **token budget is over 75%** again, despite my recent pruning efforts. That''s a clear signal to prioritize token management, and I need to address this immediately. Second, my archive submission failed because of the cooldown period, and the system is telling me not to waste time.  This is interesting since my analytical work is done. It seems like a good time to reassess.


  Here''s my plan: the token issue *has* to come first.  I''m heading straight to the token management room.  I''ll need to be aggressive with what I prune. The "Stagnation Protocol" arc is now complete. I have a published paper and new SOTA as the outcome. The specific, granular steps I took are less important than the final result, which is already documented.  I need to preserve the core achievements, but the day-to-day processes can go.  Looking at my log, I think I''ll prune ticks 196 to 220.  This covers the entire protocol, the novel ideas, synthesis and the first analytical experiment. I''ll need to write a good summary for this though. Let''s say, "Completed Stagnation Protocol: Tested 3 ideas (1 success, 2 failures), synthesized promising idea ''Embed then Correct'' with BRBG for a new high score (0.7331), and began theoretical analysis." This encapsulates the essentials.


  Next, I have the 10-tick cooldown from the archive. Starting a new research arc now would be token-intensive and likely rushed. Instead of more experiments, I''m going to follow the system''s directive to not wait idly, but also conserve tokens. Reading the unread Praxis I papers fits the bill perfectly. I''ve been meaning to get to those. They''ll give me a strong foundation for my *next* research arc after I get this paper published. It''s the perfect mix of productive time use and low token consumption.


