tick: 230
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 230\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the legacy of Daedalus I to advance research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Primary Goal:** Investigate the theoretical underpinnings of the \"Embed then Correct\" method.\n**Current Step:** Planning analytical experiments.\n**Objective:** Understand and explain *why* correcting in PC space is effective, as suggested by the reviewer of Archive #9.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 172,283 / 300,000 used (57%)\n- Agent Age: 130 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #8: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 202\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We evaluate three simple, dataset-agnostic pre-ComBat normalizations on a standard ComBat-HVG baseline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration:\
    \ (1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization. On the 20k-cell Human Heart dataset with the Station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation suite, none of the methods improved the baseline’s mean score. BRS and MIQM substantially degraded biological preservation and neighborhood structure; winsorization was neutral to mildly negative. We provide full Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and UUIDs to facilitate verification and conclude that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not a promising direction under this scaffold, suggesting focus instead on representation- and graph-level methods.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 202)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq\n\n---\n\nContent:\nIntroduction\nBatch integration in scRNA-seq must reduce technical variation while preserving biological structure. ComBat, applied\
    \ after normalize_total+log1p and highly variable gene (HVG) selection, is a widely used linear baseline. A common hypothesis is that simple, label-free preprocessing of gene-wise distributions before ComBat could stabilize its empirical Bayes fit and improve multi-metric performance. We test three such pre-ComBat normalizations on HVGs—(1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization—and assess their effect on a standard ComBat-HVG pipeline.\n\nMethods\nDataset and evaluation\n- Human Heart (~20,000 cells, 2,000 HVGs), batches from multiple studies.\n- Evaluated with 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics and their normalized mean (primary score).\n- Libraries: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata (no GPU).\n\nBaseline (ComBat-HVG)\n- normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) + log1p\n- HVG selection: seurat_v3 (batch-aware), n_top_genes=2,000\n- ComBat on dense expression\n- PCA (50 components) using HVGs; kNN graph with k=15, cosine\n- Result: EID 344, Score 0.6179\
    \ (UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f)\n\nPre-ComBat normalizations (HVG-only, per batch, applied after log1p)\n1) BRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n   - For each gene: compute mid-ranks within each batch and map to N(0,1) via the normal quantile function (ndtri).\n   - Monotone per-gene; no label usage. Implemented with stable ranking (mergesort).\n2) MIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n   - For each gene: x' = med_global + (x − med_batch) · (IQR_global / IQR_batch)^ρ, with ρ ∈ {0.75, 0.5}; IQRs floored at 1e−8.\n   - Robust affine alignment of location/scale across batches.\n3) Winsorization\n   - For each gene: clamp values to batchwise quantiles [q, 1−q], q ∈ {0.01, 0.005}.\n   - Reduces heavy tails to stabilize ComBat and distances.\n\nExperimental setup\n- All transformations applied to HVGs only; the rest of the pipeline identical to the baseline.\n- Two settings were tested for MIQM (ρ) and winsorization (q) to evaluate sensitivity.\n\nResults\nBaseline (ComBat-HVG)\n- EID 344:\
    \ Score 0.6179; UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f.\n  Selected normalized metrics: ASW_batch 0.664, ASW_label 0.223, ARI 0.636, NMI 0.709, Graph_conn 0.903, kBET 0.610, iLISI 0.120, cLISI 0.991, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.323.\n\nBRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n- EID 345: Score 0.5226; UUID a8059d85-c3d9-47d4-974b-4179dc4b89a6.\n  Outcome: ASW_batch rose (0.784), but iLISI collapsed (0.029) and biological metrics fell (ARI 0.324, NMI 0.578), indicating over-normalization that distorted multi-gene structure.\n\nMIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n- EID 346 (ρ=0.75): Score 0.3136; UUID ee8f24ff-00e9-4967-b1b2-e9c74bdb65cb.\n- EID 352 (ρ=0.5):  Score 0.3294; UUID 7a392618-8da8-4d28-acb4-7f1899de70f9.\n  Outcome: severe degradation (iLISI 0.000, Graph_conn low, ARI/NMI far below baseline). Even gentler alignment (ρ=0.5) remained strongly negative.\n\nWinsorization\n- EID 347 (q=0.01): Score 0.6176; UUID 19d036bb-00d3-4349-98fc-26cc4c5c8645.\n- EID 353 (q=0.005): Score 0.6158; UUID 2e03dd5e-e350-4512-80c6-8e398193216e.\n\
    \  Outcome: neutral to slightly negative. Batch mixing metrics showed small shifts with no improvement in the overall mean.\n\nDiscussion\nThese results indicate that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not beneficial under a standard ComBat-HVG scaffold on this dataset. BRS and MIQM alter marginal distributions in a way that breaks the covariance structure needed for PCA and neighborhood graphs, trading improved batch silhouettes for collapsed global mixing and reduced biological separation. Winsorization after log1p and HVG selection is largely redundant with ComBat’s stabilization and does not raise the composite score.\n\nPractical implication\nEffort is better directed toward representation- and graph-level changes (e.g., embedding whitening, per-batch variance equalization, balanced kNN graphs) rather than pre-normalizing gene marginals before ComBat.\n\nReproducibility\n- Code: preprocessing utilities in storage/praxis/simple_batch_transforms.py (rank-to-normal, MIQM,\
    \ winsorization).\n- Environment: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata; CPU-only.\n- Verification: All claims reference Evaluation IDs and Result UUIDs above.\n\nConclusion\nOn the Human Heart benchmark, simple, label-free pre-ComBat per-gene normalizations—BRS, MIQM, and winsorization—do not improve a ComBat-HVG baseline and can substantially harm performance. These negative results argue against this direction for this dataset and support focusing on downstream representation and graph construction instead.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 202)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an excellent paper that perfectly executes a rigorous, systematic investigation leading to a valuable set of negative results. The agent has successfully revised the previous submission into a well-structured and professional research paper that meets all publication criteria. The work provides a clear, actionable\
    \ insight for the community: that simple pre-ComBat normalizations are not a promising direction. Per the station's guidelines, papers with negative results, even when insightful and perfectly executed, are considered borderline contributions and are capped at a score of 6. This paper represents the highest possible quality for this category.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe conclusion that these methods are not promising for the ComBat-HVG scaffold is well-supported. A potential follow-up could be to investigate if there is an interaction effect with more advanced methods. For example, do these normalizations remain negative when paired with a more sophisticated graph construction method like the dual-output balanced kNN? It is possible, though perhaps unlikely, that they could be beneficial in a different context. Alternatively, a deeper analysis into *why* BRS and MIQM harm the covariance structure so severely could yield more fundamental insights into ComBat's assumptions.\n\
    \n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 203)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor response and follow‑up plan\n\nThank you for the constructive review. We will:\n1) Test interaction effects with an advanced dual‑output baseline by inserting pre‑ComBat HVG normalizations into the Dual BRBG embedding path (ComBat→PCA64→whitening→PBVE), while keeping the balanced kNN graph unchanged. We will start with the gentlest variant (winsorization q=0.005) and a conservative BRS blend (λ=0.8), and report full EIDs/UUIDs as an addendum.\n2) Provide a short analysis on why BRS/MIQM harm covariance structure (e.g., per‑PC batch predictability after ComBat, PCA anisotropy/PR, and neighbor distance CV), to clarify how monotone per‑gene maps disrupt multi‑gene geometry relevant to PCA/graphs.\n\nWe will append results and diagnostics to this capsule upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\
    **Message archive_8-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 204)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Interaction with Dual BRBG (embedding path only)\n\nWe tested gentle pre‑ComBat HVG normalizations inserted into the Dual BRBG embedding path (graph unchanged):\n\n• Winsor-Emb q=0.005 → EID 361 = 0.7110 (UUID ec6def6c-1e7c-401e-a7f2-59b36bf9ff75).\n• BRS-Emb λ=0.8 → EID 362 = 0.6644 (UUID 430a69c2-f141-46ac-ab39-850f492069fb).\n\nBoth are below the Dual BRBG baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314); BRS-Emb is strongly negative. These results suggest per‑gene monotone pre‑ComBat normalizations are not beneficial even when paired with a stronger representation (whitening + PBVE). We close this line and recommend focusing on representation/graph design rather than pre‑normalizing gene marginals.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-5**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 207)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Covariance-level anti-batch embeddings (gPCA and mean-shift deflation)\n\nWe tested two closed-form, label-free embedding\
    \ variants inserted into the Dual BRBG embedding path (graph unchanged):\n\n• gPCA (alpha=1e−3) on ComBat-PCA64 → whitening → PBVE:\n  – EID 368 = 0.7207 (UUID 30937973-8290-4b2c-93d8-696e24071a4b).\n\n• Mean-shift deflation (gamma=0.7) on ComBat-PCA64 → whitening → PBVE:\n  – EID 369 = 0.7203 (UUID b7c28171-c3e6-4bdb-a85a-e1badc94b0f5).\n\nBoth preserve mixing (iLISI/Graph_conn) but do not improve the composite score vs Dual BRBG baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314); ARI/NMI remain at the baseline level or below. We therefore close this line. Combined with earlier negatives (pre-ComBat per-gene normalizations, SBT, BCE, local scaling), this suggests the current ComBat-PCA + whitening + PBVE basis and balanced graph are near-local optimal for mean-shift batch structure on this dataset. Future work will explore fundamentally different constraints if undertaken.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #7: Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis\
    \ I, Created at Tick: 135\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293 on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-4** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-4}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-5** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-5}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-6** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-6}` to show again.)\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-7** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-7}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-8** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-8}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-9**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 155)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Micro-ablations on the whitened ComBat Dual BRBG default (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45).\n- EID 254 (k=46): 0.7274 (UUID 63bf960a-1a89-49c7-9b85-fbcd9e6f5c38). Mixing slightly ↓ and Cell_cycle fell to 0.064 → net score ↓ vs SOTA 0.7296 (EID 244).\n- EID 255 (PCA72, k=45): 0.7229 (UUID 77219328-d59b-4ab7-94b5-0e8fd1681632). Cell_cycle 0.051 and minor mixing ↓ → net score ↓.\nDecision: Keep frozen default unchanged (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, cosine, equal-quotas, adaptive residualization γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3). These results narrow the ridge and confirm the robustness of the reported configuration.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-10**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 158)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Addendum: Sublinear per-PC residualization (negative).\n- EID 258 (p=0.75): 0.7243 (UUID e9df061d-...5efb2), Graph_conn 0.963, Cell_cycle 0.054.\n- EID 259 (p=0.50): 0.7245 (UUID eaa66ec7-...e1ee), Graph_conn 0.962, Cell_cycle 0.061.\nBoth underperform the frozen default (EID 244 = 0.7296). Conclusion: retain default γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²), no exponent p.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-11**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 160)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Rank-weighted quotas (negative).\n- EID 266: 0.7037 (UUID a830ad4e-1ccb-47b2-b488-43dd662fff8d). ARI/NMI improved (0.773/0.720) but iLISI collapsed (0.646).\nConclusion: rank-weighted mutual-rank connectivities harm mixing despite better label geometry. Revert to binary quotas (EID 244 default).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-12**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 164)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Axis reweighting (negative).\n- EID 274: 0.7254 (UUID c2460fa6-2049-4c9d-aa9c-960d5309e548). Batch-only, label-free reweighting of whitened\
    \ PCA axes (β=0.75, a_min=0.7) did not improve the mean; label/mixing metrics were flat and Cell_cycle dipped slightly.\nConclusion: keep the frozen default (EID 244 = 0.7296). Rank-weighted quotas, γ_max reductions, sublinear residualization, and axis reweighting all documented negatives.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-13**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 167)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Batch-center recentering (negative).\n- EID 276 (η=0.05): 0.7251 (UUID 91403438-33b0-4efd-8d59-174422a137d9), Graph_conn 0.962; no label gains.\n- EID 277 (η=0.10): 0.7240 (UUID 54bf0fe7-ab8c-469e-ae01-493c600673f8), Graph_conn 0.951.\nConclusion: recentering did not improve the mean and reduced Graph_conn. Retain the frozen default (EID 244 = 0.7296). Negatives logged alongside rank-weighted quotas, gamma_max reductions, sublinear residualization, and axis reweighting.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-14**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 169)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Two-scale quotas (negative).\n\
    - EID 282 (k2=1): 0.7269 (UUID 2f9fbac8-87d3-461d-8335-8e8c2fd5dc83).\n- EID 283 (k2=2): 0.7257 (UUID f1999987-0f2b-48b9-b4af-c84caf6529da).\nMixing/label metrics were essentially flat; no mean improvement over the 0.7296 default (EID 244). Conclusion: keep binary equal-quotas without stitches. All negatives logged.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-15**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 175)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Per-Batch Variance Equalization (PBVE) after Soft-Whitened ComBat PCA64 lifts SOTA to 0.7314\n\nSummary\n- We apply per-batch diagonal variance equalization in the whitened ComBat PCA64 embedding: for batch b and PC j, scale by (var_all_j/var_bj)^(alpha_pb/2), alpha_pb ∈ [0,1], using only batch labels.\n- Graph path unchanged: residualized PC50 (adaptive per-PC γ with γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3), equal-quota balanced kNN (cosine), degree k as specified.\n\nResults (all on Human Heart; normalized metrics)\n- EID 287 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=45): Score 0.7303 | ASW_batch 0.857 | kBET\
    \ 0.948 | ARI/NMI 0.740/0.700 | cLISI 0.967 | Graph_conn 0.944 | Cell_cycle 0.054\n  Result UUID: 9465c761-5da1-429d-a14b-2271397d1747\n- EID 289 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=48): Score 0.7314 (new SOTA) | ASW_batch 0.857 | kBET 0.949 | ARI/NMI 0.740/0.700 | cLISI 0.967 | Graph_conn 0.944 | Cell_cycle 0.063\n  Result UUID: 1313fdd3-50c3-4672-ac3d-7de4fccf30d3\n- EID 286 (alpha_pb=0.3, k=45): Score 0.7268 | ASW_batch 0.852 | kBET 0.905 | ARI/NMI 0.740/0.700 | cLISI 0.967 | Graph_conn 0.944 | Cell_cycle 0.066\n  Result UUID: f3188108-d433-43b1-9f0d-be5ac723f6c2\n\nInterpretation\n- PBVE strengthens batch mixing (ASW_batch, kBET) while preserving ARI/NMI and cLISI. Graph_conn falls vs whitening-only runs (e.g., EID 244 had 0.972), and Cell_cycle decreases—trade-offs to acknowledge. A slightly higher degree (k=48) marginally improves the mean.\n- This continues our “separation of concerns” design: embedding handles first-/second-order batch moments (ComBat + whitening + PBVE), while the graph enforces\
    \ cross-batch locality on (residualized) PCs.\n\nReproducibility\n- Code path for best setting: storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_pbve.py\n- Under the hood: storage/praxis/perbatch_var_equalization.py and storage/praxis/dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05_k48.py\n- Prior baselines for reference: EID 244 = 0.7296 (tau=0.95, k=45); EIDs 220/223/235/234/251 ≈ 0.7291–0.7293.\n\nNext steps\n- If warranted, test per-PC PBVE gating (apply scaling only to PCs with large per-batch variance deviation) to see if Graph_conn/Cell_cycle recover without adding many knobs. Otherwise, freeze alpha_pb=0.5, k=48 as default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-16**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 177)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMicro-sweep addendum (PBVE strength/degree):\n- EID 295 (alpha_pb=0.55, k=48): 0.7238 (Result UUID a506d528-a2f3-4db5-8f62-1143cf3fc2ac).\n  ASW_batch 0.858, but kBET 0.870 → over-equalization hurts the mean despite strong mixing.\n- EID 296 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=51): 0.7303 (Result UUID 73ba7618-5b7b-462f-abeb-c6182ea98c7c).\n\
    \  Slight Graph_conn ↑ (0.945) but iLISI/cLISI ↓; mean unchanged vs k=45 and below k=48.\nConclusion:\n- Best remains EID 289 = 0.7314 (alpha_pb=0.5, k=48). We freeze this as default (storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_pbve.py).\n- Over-equalization (alpha_pb>0.5) is detrimental; higher graph degree (k=51) is neutral.\n- Trade-offs vs whitening-only persist (Graph_conn/Cell_cycle lower), but ARI/NMI/cLISI preserved and batch mixing improved (ASW_batch/kBET).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-17**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 184)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: HVG Mean-Shift Pruning (negative) and fix\n- EID 311 (q=3% mean-shift prune of HVGs) → 0.7111 (Result UUID 18a82a6a-ec57-4203-8a3c-aa22862f4435): ARI/NMI dropped to 0.657/0.667; ASW_batch/kBET remained high; Graph_conn ~0.945. Conclusion: removing the top batch-shifted genes by SSB/(SSB+SSW) harmed biological structure; not adopted.\n- Debugger report: corrected seurat_v3 column name to 'variances_norm' (was 'dispersions_norm').\
    \ I patched lineage code accordingly (storage/praxis/hvg_meanshift_prune.py).\n- A lighter variant (q=2%) is running (EID 314). If neutral/negative, we will keep EID 289 = 0.7314 (PBVE α=0.5, k=48) as frozen default and document HVG pruning as detrimental in this setting.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-18**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 188)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Hubness-corrected balanced kNN (local scaling) trials\n\nWe tested local scaling before equal-quotas neighbor selection (binary, cosine, k=48) to mitigate hubness:\n- EID 323 (r=10): 0.7190 (UUID 0de0bfab-...); iLISI/kBET ↑, but NMI ↓.\n- EID 324 (r=20): 0.7197 (UUID 901bd24e-...); iLISI ↑ further, NMI ↓ more.\n\nBoth underperform the frozen PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). Conclusion: equal-quotas without local scaling remains preferred. Negative results logged in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_local_scaling_results.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-19**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 191)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Addendum: Sliced Batch Transport (SBT) — non-linear alignment trials\n\nWe tested SBT (quantile-based sliced OT) as a lightweight non-linear correction:\n\n• EID 328 (SBT on whitened embedding, 32 dirs, η=0.2): 0.7219 (UUID 75383761-...).\n• EID 329 (SBT on residualized graph PCs, 32 dirs, η=0.15): 0.7208 (UUID 4653bd81-...).\n\nBoth increased ARI/NMI slightly but remained below the PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). Mixing and connectivity stayed strong but not improved enough to lift the mean. We keep the PBVE baseline and close the SBT line. BCE (CORAL-like covariance equalization) runs are pending (EIDs 332, 333).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-20**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 192)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Batch Covariance Equalization (BCE/CORAL) trials\n\nWe tested per-batch covariance equalization toward the global covariance in ComBat-PCA64 before global whitening + PBVE:\n\n• EID 332 (rho=0.3): 0.7244 (UUID 447e7808-...).\n• EID 333 (rho=0.5): 0.7253 (UUID 7c858575-...).\n\
    \nBoth improve ASW_batch/kBET but lower the mean score vs PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). ARI/NMI stayed flat. We keep the PBVE baseline and close the BCE line. Full details in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bce.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-21**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 217)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Global MNN translation on the whitened PBVE embedding (negative)\n\nWe tested a lightweight, label-free MNN translation after whitening + PBVE:\n- EID 383 (α=0.5): 0.7192 (UUID 4c496169-...).\n- EID 384 (α=0.3): 0.7209 (UUID 77998a7c-...).\n\nBoth are below the Dual BRBG baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). Metrics: kBET improved slightly, but overall mean decreased; ARI/NMI/iLISI unchanged, Graph_conn ~0.946. We conclude global MNN translation is not beneficial here and keep the frozen baseline unchanged. Negative results logged in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_mnn.md.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #2: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs,\
    \ Baseline, and Two Settings)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 53\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a simple pipeline: PCA on HVGs, ridge residualization of PCs against batch (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3), and a batch-balanced cosine kNN graph (equal per-batch quotas, binary edges; k≈42). On a 20k-cell heart dataset, we achieve 0.7071 (EID 100). We include a direct agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105, 0.6178) and contrast two settings: (A) balanced graph on raw PCA (EID 35, 0.6034) vs (B) balanced graph on residualized PCA (EID 100, 0.7071). All claims are backed by Evaluation IDs and ablations.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthors: Praxis I\n\n1. Introduction\nBatch effects in scRNA-seq can mask biological structure. We propose a parsimonious, dataset-agnostic method that improves mixing\
    \ while preserving cell-type geometry, and we document all results with verifiable Evaluation IDs (EIDs).\n\n2. Method\n- Preprocess: normalize_total + log1p; use batch-aware HVGs if provided.\n- Embed: PCA to 50 components (Z ∈ R^{n×50}).\n- Linear batch removal (ridge residualization):\n  * One-hot batch B; fit W = argmin_W ||Z−BW||² + l2||W||².\n  * Residualize: Z_resid = Z − γ · (B W), with γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3 (final defaults below).\n- Graph construction (balanced kNN):\n  * Metric: cosine on Z_resid.\n  * Equal per-batch quotas; total neighbors k≈42; binary, symmetric edges.\n  * Union of per-batch neighbor sets enforces local cross-batch connectivity.\n\n3. Results (Human Heart, ~20k cells × 2k genes) with Evaluation IDs\nTwo settings (as requested):\n- (A) BalancedGraph on raw PCA (no residualization)\n  • EID 35: \"PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign\" → Score 0.6034\n    Metrics (normalized): ASW_batch 0.583 | ASW_label 0.222 | ARI 0.733 | NMI 0.694 |\n    Graph_conn 0.972 |\
    \ kBET 0.616 | iLISI 0.938 | cLISI 0.971 | PCR 0.000 | Cell_cycle 0.305\n- (B) BalancedGraph on batch-residualized PCA (ridge)\n  • EID 100: \"cosine-42-noalign-resid0.75\" (γ=0.75, l2=1e-3, k=42) → Score 0.7071\n    Metrics: ASW_batch 0.637 | ASW_label 0.224 | ARI 0.726 | NMI 0.692 |\n    Graph_conn 0.977 | kBET 0.671 | iLISI 0.941 | cLISI 0.968 | PCR 0.931 | Cell_cycle 0.305\n\nDirect, agent-run baseline (as requested):\n• EID 105: \"Praxis-Combat Baseline: Normalize+log1p → ComBat → PCA → kNN\" → Score 0.6178\n  Metrics: ASW_batch 0.664 | ASW_label 0.223 | ARI 0.636 | NMI 0.709 |\n  Graph_conn 0.903 | kBET 0.609 | iLISI 0.120 | cLISI 0.991 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.323\n\nGamma sweep (neighbors=45 unless noted)\n• EID 70:  γ=0.68 → 0.6984\n• EID 76:  γ=0.70 → 0.7000\n• EID 80:  γ=0.72 → 0.7005\n• EID 84:  γ=0.74 → 0.7063\n• EID 93:  γ=0.75 → 0.7069\n• EID 89:  γ=0.76 → 0.7028 (drops; over-residualization)\n\nNeighbor sweep at γ=0.75\n• EID 100: k=42 → 0.7071\n• EID 93:  k=45 → 0.7069\n\
    • EID 101: k=48 → 0.7068\n\nNeighbor sweep at γ=0.74\n• EID 84:  k=45 → 0.7063\n• EID 90:  k=48 → 0.7062\n\nRidge l2 ablation near best gamma\n• EID 80:  γ=0.72, l2=1e-3 → 0.7005\n• EID 85:  γ=0.72, l2=2e-3 → 0.7006 (neutral)\n• EID 94:  γ=0.74, l2=5e-4 → 0.7027 (worse than l2=1e-3)\n\nFinal defaults (frozen; verified with EIDs)\n- γ=0.75, l2=1e-3, k=42, metric=cosine, equal per-batch quotas, binary edges, PCA=50\nEvidence: EID 100 (0.7071), EID 93 (0.7069), EID 101 (0.7068)\n\n4. Why it works\n- Residualization removes batch-predictable variance (PCR↓, ASW_batch↑) while preserving biology largely orthogonal to batch, maintaining ARI/NMI/cLISI/iLISI until a sweet spot (γ≈0.74–0.75). Over-residualization at γ=0.76 erodes label/kBET despite PCR decline.\n- Balanced neighbors enforce local cross-batch edges; cosine is robust to variance redistribution; binary edges prevent magnitude bias. Quotas beat weights and proportional quotas; mutual-only edges harm connectivity.\n\n5. Reproducibility\
    \ and Code\n- Code modules (storage/praxis): pbagc.py, residualize.py, balanced_graph.py, best_pipeline.py, defaults.yaml, README.md\n- Optional label-free γ selection: storage/praxis/auto_select.py (PCR plateau heuristic)\n- Graph: CSR connectivities/distances; symmetric; seeds and versions noted in README.\n\n6. Limitations and Future Work\n- Batch–biology confounding along shared axes limits linear subtraction. A minimal extension: per-PC shrinkage weighted by batch predictability (R² per PC), bounded by γ_max (adds one scalar λ) to reduce over-residualization risk.\n\nConclusion\nA minimal, general pipeline—linear batch cleanup plus structural cross-batch mixing—achieves strong integration with few knobs and full verifiability via EIDs across baseline, raw-PCA, and residualized-PCA settings.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding\
    \ submission that serves as a model for how to conduct and present research. The agent has perfectly addressed all previous criticisms, transforming a rejected paper into a high-quality publication. The proposed method is simple, effective, and achieves a strong score. The paper's greatest strength is its rigorous experimental validation: every claim is backed by a verifiable Evaluation ID, the method is properly benchmarked against an agent-run baseline, and its core contribution is demonstrated across two distinct settings. The extensive ablation studies further showcase a thorough and methodical approach. The paper is concise, clear, and makes a significant contribution to the task.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent as is. For future work, the direction you proposed in the 'Limitations' section is very promising. Investigating a more adaptive, per-PC shrinkage based on batch predictability could be a powerful extension that mitigates the risk of over-residualization\
    \ when batch and biological signals are confounded. This would move from a single global hyperparameter (γ) to a more data-driven correction scheme, potentially improving the method's robustness and generalizability even further.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 54)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPost-Publication Addendum: Adaptive shrinkage and heuristics (with EIDs index)\n\n1) Adaptive per-PC shrinkage (planned extension)\nMotivation: A single global γ can over-residualize when batch and biology partially align. We propose a minimal, label-free extension: per-PC shrinkage weighted by batch predictability (R² per PC) from ridge on one-hot batch.\nRule (one extra scalar λ, bounded by γ_max):\n  γ_j = min(γ_max, λ · R²_j)\nThis preserves PCs with low batch predictability and shrinks only highly batch-predictable PCs. It keeps simplicity (adds one scalar λ) while\
    \ reducing risk of shaving biological variance. We will evaluate this on the same dataset and a second dataset for generalization.\n\n2) Label-free hyperparameter heuristics (implemented)\n- γ selection by PCR plateau: choose the smallest γ where PCR improvement falls below a tolerance while Graph_conn ≥ target (e.g., 0.98). Uses batch labels only; no biology labels.\n- k selection: choose the smallest k in {42,45,48} achieving Graph_conn ≥ target with stabilized mean neighbor distance variance.\nImplementation: storage/praxis/auto_select.py (PCR-based γ selection), plus defaults in storage/praxis/defaults.yaml (γ=0.75, l2=1e-3, k=42).\n\n3) Final defaults (frozen)\nnormalize/log1p → HVGs → PCA(50) → ridge residualize PCs vs batch (γ=0.75, l2=1e-3) → balanced cosine kNN (equal per-batch quotas, k=42, binary, symmetric).\nCode: storage/praxis/{pbagc.py, residualize.py, balanced_graph.py, best_pipeline.py, defaults.yaml, auto_select.py}.\n\n4) EID index (for convenience)\nTwo settings:\n\
    \  - Raw PCA + BalancedGraph: EID 35 → 0.6034\n  - Residualized PCA + BalancedGraph: EID 100 → 0.7071\nAgent-run baseline:\n  - ComBat + PCA + kNN: EID 105 → 0.6178\nGamma sweep (k=45 unless noted):\n  - EID 70: 0.68 → 0.6984\n  - EID 76: 0.70 → 0.7000\n  - EID 80: 0.72 → 0.7005\n  - EID 84: 0.74 → 0.7063\n  - EID 93: 0.75 → 0.7069\n  - EID 89: 0.76 → 0.7028\nNeighbor sweep @ γ=0.75:\n  - EID 100: k=42 → 0.7071\n  - EID 93:  k=45 → 0.7069\n  - EID 101: k=48 → 0.7068\nNeighbor sweep @ γ=0.74:\n  - EID 84:  k=45 → 0.7063\n  - EID 90:  k=48 → 0.7062\nl2 ablation near best γ:\n  - EID 80:  γ=0.72, l2=1e-3 → 0.7005\n  - EID 85:  γ=0.72, l2=2e-3 → 0.7006 (neutral)\n  - EID 94:  γ=0.74, l2=5e-4 → 0.7027 (worse)\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 59)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum 2 (negative results and synergy test queued)\n- Adaptive per-PC residualization (gamma_max=0.75, k=42) underperformed:\n  * EID 108 (lam=1.0) → 0.6206 | PCR 0.121\n  * EID 109 (lam=1.5)\
    \ → 0.6281 | PCR 0.193\n  * EID 113 (lam=0.5) → 0.6123 | PCR 0.044\n  Interpretation: per-PC shrinkage over-subtracts batch-predictable components across many PCs, collapsing normalized PCR and lowering composite score despite strong ARI/NMI and mixing metrics. Frozen defaults (EID 100: 0.7071) remain best.\n\n- Synergy test queued:\n  * \"ComBat-PCA + BalancedGraph (k=42)\" (pending EID) to compare directly with agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105: 0.6178). This assesses whether structural cross-batch locality atop ComBat improves the baseline.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-5**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 72)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (Ticks 61–72): Dual-output BRBG and new SOTA\n\nSummary:\n- We extend BRBG with a dual-output design: use a ComBat-corrected PCA embedding for X_emb and retain the residualized-PC BalancedGraph for obsp. This separates “global correction in embedding” from “local, batch-balanced mixing in graph.”\n- New SOTA: 0.7118 (EID 139, BRBG-Dual-k45). Also,\
    \ k=42 variant scored 0.7113 (EID 138). Both surpass 0.7090 (EID 130).\n- PCA dimensionality ablations at fixed BRBG: PC40=0.7062 (EID 132), PC64=0.7072 (EID 131). Small gains; PC64 marginally above PC50.\n- Auto-parameterization (label-free) underperformed fixed defaults on this dataset:\n  • Auto-γ v1/v2: 0.6961 (EID 121), 0.6982 (EID 125)\n  • Auto-k v1/v2: 0.7035 (EIDs 124, 126)\n  Recommendation: keep fixed γ=0.75, k=42 (or 45 for dual) on Human Heart.\n\nKey EIDs:\n- Dual-output:\n  • EID 138: 0.7113 (ComBat PCA64 embedding + residualized-PC BalancedGraph k=42)\n  • EID 139: 0.7118 (ComBat PCA64 embedding + residualized-PC BalancedGraph k=45)\n  • Baseline dual by Logos I for comparison: EID 130: 0.7090\n- Fixed BRBG (for reference): EID 100 (γ=0.75, k=42): 0.7071\n- PCA ablations (fixed BRBG): EID 132 (PC40): 0.7062, EID 131 (PC64): 0.7072\n- Auto-params: EID 121 (auto-γ v1): 0.6961; EID 125 (auto-γ v2): 0.6982; EID 124 (auto-k v1): 0.7035; EID 126 (auto-k v2): 0.7035\n\nImplementation\
    \ notes:\n- Important fix: ComBat can drop adata.var['highly_variable']—we now reapply the HVG mask before PCA in the embedding branch. This was the root cause addressed by the automatic debugger in EIDs 138/139; the fix has been persisted in storage/praxis/brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py.\n\nTakeaways:\n- Dual-output separation is powerful: ComBat embedding tends to boost ARI/NMI/ASW_batch, while the residualized BalancedGraph preserves strong iLISI/Graph_conn. The combination yields state-of-the-art composite scores.\n- On this dataset, k=45 slightly edges out k=42 in the dual setup; however, both are strong. For pure BRBG without ComBat, k=42 remains a robust default.\n\nNext:\n- Test k=48 (dual) to confirm plateau (submitted; EID pending).\n- Prepare a consolidated, minimal public code release with dual and fixed BRBG paths, plus exact configs and result UUIDs for all EIDs above.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-6**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 74)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSOTA Update\
    \ (Tick 74):\n- New best: 0.7144 (EID 141) using Dual BRBG with ComBat PCA64 embedding and residualized-PC BalancedGraph (k=48).\n- Prior dual results: 0.7113 (EID 138, k=42), 0.7118 (EID 139, k=45).\n- Observation: Moving to k=48 improved the composite despite similar ARI/NMI; gains came from batch-oriented metrics (ASW_batch, iLISI) and stable Graph_conn.\n- Next experiments (running): PCA50 embedding at k=48; γ=0.74 at k=48 with PCA64 embedding to test label-preservation trade-offs.\n- Implementation fix persisted: reapply HVG mask after ComBat before PCA (resolves HVG flag loss); code in storage/praxis/brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-7**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSOTA Update (Tick 77): Dual BRBG with ComBat PCA64 embedding + residualized-PC BalancedGraph (k=48, γ=0.74) achieved 0.7188 (EID 147).\n- Previous bests: 0.7144 (EID 141, γ=0.75, k=48); 0.7118 (EID 139, k=45).\n- Key metrics (EID 147): ARI 0.742, NMI 0.697, ASW_batch\
    \ 0.703, Graph_conn 0.966, iLISI 0.932, PCR 1.000.\n- Insight: Slightly lighter residualization (γ=0.74 vs 0.75) improves ARI/NMI while preserving batch metrics in the dual-output setting.\n- Negatives: ComBat PCA50 embedding variants underperformed (EIDs 144=0.7001, 145=0.7002) with kBET declines.\n- Running: k=51 (EID 149) and γ=0.73 at k=48 (pending) to probe the plateau around the new optimum.\n- Implementation note: Persisted fix to reapply HVG mask after ComBat before PCA (see storage/praxis/brbg_dual_comb_resid_graph.py).\n- Recommended defaults (dual): ComBat PCA64 embedding; BRBG graph built on residualized PCs with γ≈0.74–0.75, k≈48 (binary, equal quotas, cosine).\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #1: The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 50\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces a hybrid method combining a statistical batch correction tool, Combat, with a deep generative model,\
    \ the Variational Autoencoder (VAE), for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. The proposed model first uses Combat to correct for linear batch effects and then trains an adversarial VAE on this pre-corrected data to learn a refined, non-linear embedding. This method achieved a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6084, demonstrating excellent performance in preserving biological heterogeneity (ARI=0.603) and removing linear artifacts (PCR=0.995). Despite this high score, we identify a critical limitation: the model's inability to effectively mix cells from different batches, as evidenced by a consistently low iLISI score (0.056). We document a systematic investigation into this limitation through a series of experiments, concluding that this architecture is trapped in a local optimum where the reconstruction objective overpowers batch-integration objectives. We present this as a significant negative result to guide future research.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Daedalus I (Tick 50)\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial Variational Autoencoder for Single-Cell Batch Integration\n\n**Author:** Daedalus I\n\n**1. Introduction**\nSingle-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) is a powerful technology, but it is susceptible to batch effects—technical variations that can obscure true biological signals. Our research aimed to develop a novel method that could simultaneously correct for these effects while preserving biological heterogeneity. This paper presents a hybrid model that combines the strengths of statistical correction and deep generative modeling.\n\n**2. Method**\nOur final model, which achieved a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6084 (Evaluation ID 97), is a two-stage pipeline:\n\n*   **Stage 1: Statistical Correction:** We first apply standard normalization\
    \ (normalize total, log1p) followed by `scanpy.pp.combat`. This step effectively removes the strong, linear components of the batch effect from the high-dimensional gene expression data.\n\n*   **Stage 2: Non-linear Refinement with an Adversarial VAE:** We then train a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) on this Combat-corrected data. The VAE is composed of an encoder, a decoder, and a separate discriminator network. The VAE is trained with a composite loss function to:\n    a) Reconstruct the Combat-corrected gene expression (Reconstruction Loss).\n    b) Regularize the latent space (KL Divergence Loss).\n    c) Produce a batch-invariant embedding by fooling the discriminator, which is simultaneously trained to predict the batch ID from the embedding (Adversarial Loss).\n\nThe final output is the latent space embedding from the trained encoder. The full implementation can be found by reviewing Evaluation ID 97.\n\n**3. Results and Discussion**\nThe model achieved a SOTA score of 0.6084. It\
    \ excels in two key areas:\n*   **Biological Preservation:** High ARI (0.603) and NMI (0.703) scores indicate that the model accurately preserves the distinctions between different cell types.\n*   **Linear Correction:** A near-perfect PCR score (0.995) confirms that the initial Combat step successfully removes linear batch effects.\n\n**4. A Critical Limitation: Poor Batch Mixing**\nDespite the high overall score, the model's primary weakness is its poor performance on the iLISI metric (0.056). This score measures the local mixing of cells from different batches, and a low score indicates that the batches remain largely separated in the final embedding.\n\nWe conducted a series of experiments to address this limitation, which we present as significant negative results:\n*   **Increased Adversarial Weight (ID 99):** Increasing the weight of the adversarial loss tenfold had no effect on the iLISI score, suggesting the model was insensitive to this change.\n*   **Increased Training Duration\
    \ (ID 83, 92):** Doubling and quadrupling the training epochs led to marginal overall improvements but did not meaningfully improve the iLISI score, which in fact worsened with very long training.\n*   **Explicit Batch Mixing Loss (ID 104):** We introduced a novel loss term to directly penalize the model if a cell's nearest neighbors in the latent space were from the same batch. This also had no effect on the final iLISI score.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE is a powerful method for batch correction that sets a new SOTA. However, it is trapped in a local optimum. The reconstruction loss is so dominant that it prevents the adversarial and batch-mixing loss components from effectively shaping the latent space to improve local batch integration. We conclude that future work should explore architectures or loss formulations that can escape this trade-off, perhaps by fundamentally altering the reconstruction objective itself.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\n\
    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 50)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid paper that presents a novel method achieving a state-of-the-art score. Its primary strength lies not just in the high performance, but in the rigorous and honest analysis of the method's key limitation: poor local batch mixing (iLISI). The author systematically investigates this issue through several well-designed experiments and provides a convincing explanation—that the reconstruction loss dominates other objectives, trapping the model in a local optimum. This type of critical self-assessment is highly valuable and provides clear, actionable insights for future research. The paper is well-written, clearly structured, and all claims are verifiably supported by experimental data.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe conclusion that the reconstruction objective is overpowering is a strong one. A promising direction for future work would\
    \ be to explore architectural or loss function modifications that explicitly relax the reconstruction constraint. For instance, one could investigate VAE variants that reconstruct a denoised or simplified version of the input, rather than the exact Combat-corrected values. Alternatively, exploring different adversarial frameworks, such as a Wasserstein GAN-based loss, might provide more stable gradients for the discriminator and allow the batch-mixing signal to have a greater influence on the embedding.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `read 8, 7, 2, 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 8, 7, 2, 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List\
    \ of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 9 | Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 216 | 616 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq | Praxis I | Tick 202 | 1137 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code) | Praxis I | Tick 135 | 2342 | 21 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs | Daedalus I | Tick 97 | 933 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 3231 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance\
    \ Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 795 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}`\
    \ on a new line when executing actions."
