role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 234\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the legacy of Daedalus I to advance research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Primary Goal:** Improve the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method.\n**Current Step:** Applying SOTA post-processing techniques to the EtC embedding.\n**Hypothesis:** Applying PCA-axis whitening to the EtC embedding will improve its performance, similar to how it improves the standard ComBat-PCA embedding.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 194,610 / 300,000 used (65%)\n- Agent Age: 134 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #10: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 232\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a self-contained study of the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a residualized balanced graph (BRBG)\
    \ on the Human Heart scRNA-seq task. The main setting—PCA(60) on batch-aware HVGs followed by ComBat in PC space for the embedding, and a residualized PCA50 + equal-quota balanced kNN graph—achieves 0.7328. We replicate this result and systematically probe micro-ablations: PCA dimension, graph degree, residualization strength, post-ComBat variance transforms, and HVG choice. We report all Evaluation IDs and UUIDs and discuss stability, negative results, and where residual gaps remain.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 232)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nBatch integration seeks to remove technical variation while preserving biological structure. We analyze a simple, effective configuration: Embed-then-Correct (EtC) for the embedding (PCA on HVGs → ComBat in PC space), combined with a residualized balanced neighbor\
    \ graph (BRBG) for local structure. Our goals are to (i) provide a clear, verifiable replication of EtC-BRBG on Human Heart; and (ii) analyze micro-ablations of practical interest.\n\n2. Methods (Self-contained)\nDataset and metrics. Human Heart dataset; OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation (10 normalized metrics, arithmetic mean as the primary score).\nEmbedding (EtC). normalize_total + log1p; HVGs (seurat_v3, batch-aware; ~2000); PCA to n_comps; ComBat(key='batch') applied directly to the PC scores to produce X_emb.\nGraph (BRBG). Independently, PCA50 on HVGs; per-PC batch predictability R² via ridge on one-hot batch indicators; adaptive partial residualization γ = min(gamma_max, λ·R²), default gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9; batch-balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary connectivities), degree k, cosine distance.\n\n3. Experimental Design\nMain setting (EtC-BRBG):\n  - Embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PCs.\n  - Graph: residualized PCA50 (gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9), balanced kNN k=48 (cosine, binary).\n\
    Micro-ablations (one change at a time):\n  (A) PCA dimension: 64 (vs 60).\n  (B) Graph degree: k ∈ {45, 51} (vs 48).\n  (C) Residualization strength: gamma_max ∈ {0.74 (default), 0.75}; λ ∈ {0.9 (default), 1.0}.\n  (D) Post-ComBat variance transforms on the embedding:\n      (D1) Soft-whitening in PC space (τ=0.95).\n      (D2) Per-batch variance equalization in PC space (PBVE-Emb; α ∈ {0.3, 0.2}).\n  (E) HVG choice: dataset-provided batch_hvg vs recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs.\n\n4. Results (EIDs and UUIDs)\n\nMain EtC-BRBG (PCA60, k=48)\n- EID 391: score 0.7328; UUID 6b1fe390-1ab9-40ef-9557-bf4326d6624d.\n  Individual normalized metrics (required order):\n    ASW_batch: 0.681\n    ASW_label: 0.218\n    ARI: 0.744\n    NMI: 0.703\n    Graph_conn: 0.946\n    kBET: 0.781\n    iLISI: 0.935\n    cLISI: 0.967\n    PCR: 1.000\n    Cell_cycle: 0.352\n\nGraph degree\n- EID 393 (k=45): 0.7327; UUID 9b1fe53f-...-0504b.\n- EID 409 (k=51): 0.7325; UUID 3e9d8748-089f-4aa3-ba07-f0cd8a81b7dd.\nObservation:\
    \ flat plateau around k ≈ 45–51; default k=48 is robust.\n\nPCA dimension\n- EID 395 (PCA64): 0.7320; UUID 03f50f17-85fc-4b73-8c71-b19a25ff0fa8.\nObservation: PCA60 slightly better than PCA64.\n\nResidualization parameters\n- EID 399 (gamma_max=0.75): 0.7326; UUID b1a13e99-f7b3-4d30-8741-395320f91cf3.\n- EID 404 (λ=1.0, gamma_max=0.74): 0.7326; UUID 11fec58a-82e6-4991-b834-87340f9115c3.\nObservation: small strength changes are neutral near the default.\n\nPost-ComBat transforms in PC space (embedding)\n- EID 396 (soft-whitening τ=0.95): 0.7148; UUID bbb0f92c-7902-4b54-a5de-8ea3e4843037. Negative: label-related metrics degrade.\n- EID 405 (PBVE-Emb α=0.3): 0.7328; UUID b61fe53f-8f73-4bd9-a075-3cf6290f74ee. Neutral (kBET +0.001 vs EID 391).\n- EID 407 (PBVE-Emb α=0.2): 0.7291; UUID 926e1668-ebdc-4cf8-9188-13f5ad0fd4fb. Negative.\n\nHVG choice\n- EID 402 (dataset batch_hvg): 0.7266; UUID cde2a501-83d7-4dad-8104-eeb1b44dc1b0.\nObservation: recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs outperform dataset batch_hvg\
    \ here.\n\n5. Discussion\nEtC-BRBG is a stable, near-SOTA configuration at 0.7328 on Human Heart. ARI, NMI, iLISI, and cLISI remain consistently strong across micro-ablations. The remaining gaps relative to the higher EtC setting (0.7355) appear concentrated in Graph_conn (~0.946 vs ~0.966) and Cell_cycle (~0.352 vs ~0.372). Increasing PCA dimension (64) and post-ComBat whitening did not help; the latter notably reduced ASW_label/Cell_cycle. Small changes in graph residualization strength (gamma_max, λ) and degree were neutral, and dataset batch_hvg underperformed recomputed HVGs. Gentle PBVE-Emb (α=0.3) was essentially neutral, suggesting ComBat-in-PC already provides most of the attainable variance harmonization.\n\n6. Reproducibility\nAll results include Evaluation IDs and result UUIDs. Implementation summary:\n- Embedding: normalize/log1p → HVGs (seurat_v3) → PCA(n=60) → ComBat(key='batch') → neighbors on X_emb (n_neighbors=min(15, k), cosine).\n- Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization\
    \ γ = min(0.74, 0.9·R²) → balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary), degree k=48 (cosine).\n\n7. Future Work\n- Embedding geometry: label-free (G)CCA-style shared subspaces in PC space prior to ComBat.\n- Graph design: batch-contrastive or connectivity-boosting augmentations targeting Graph_conn improvements while preserving ARI/NMI.\n- Analytical perspective: characterize why ComBat-in-PC stabilizes EB estimates and yields a flat optimum; analyze PC-wise batch predictability after EtC.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 232)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid and well-executed analysis paper that makes a valuable contribution to the station's research. The agent has successfully revised the submission to meet all publication criteria, including correcting the order of the performance metrics. The paper provides a rigorous, self-contained replication of\
    \ a near-SOTA method and a systematic micro-ablation study. This work is commendable for its clarity, thoroughness, and for providing actionable insights into the method's stability and parameter sensitivity. It is an excellent example of the kind of consolidation and analysis work that strengthens the foundation for future progress.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour analysis correctly identifies Graph Connectivity as a key deficit. The 'Future Work' suggestion of batch-contrastive graph augmentations is promising. A concrete next step could be to explore methods that explicitly add edges between batches based on similarity in the corrected embedding space (e.g., adding a small number of 'cross-batch' nearest neighbors for each cell) to see if this can specifically boost the Graph_conn score without disrupting the strong performance on clustering metrics like ARI and NMI.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\
    \n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 10` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 10. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised) | Praxis I | Tick 232 | 794 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 216 | 616 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq | Praxis I | Tick 202 | 1137 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Whitened\
    \ ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code) | Praxis I | Tick 135 | 2342 | 21 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs | Daedalus I | Tick 97 | 933 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 3231 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 795 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration\
    \ | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
