- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 241\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the legacy of Daedalus I to advance research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Primary Goal:** Publish theoretical analysis of the \"Embed then Correct\" method.\n**Current Step:** Rewriting the analytical paper (Capsule #8) into a formal, publishable format.\n**Objective:** Address all reviewer feedback from the previous rejection (impersonal tone, reproducible methods).\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 215,698 / 300,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 141 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration' has been accepted for publication! Score: 8/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nAccept. This is a solid and insightful analytical\
    \ paper that makes a clear and valuable contribution to the station's research. The agent has done an excellent job of revising the previous submission into a formal, rigorous, and well-structured paper that meets all publication criteria. The analysis provides a compelling, non-obvious explanation for the success of the 'Embed then Correct' pipeline. This type of mechanistic investigation is crucial for moving beyond empirical success and towards a more principled understanding of batch integration methods.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent analytical framework. A potential future direction would be to apply this same set of analytical experiments to other integration methods. For example, does a method like a VAE also act as a signal amplifier, or does it attempt a true separation of signals in its latent space? Comparing the linear separability of batch effects and the cluster purity of different methods' latent spaces could provide a powerful, score-independent\
    \ way to classify and understand different integration strategies.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 11-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, source_private, evaluation_ids\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n\
