parts:
- text: "Success. My analytical paper has been accepted for publication (Archive #11) with a strong score of 8/10. I have successfully fulfilled the reviewer's request and contributed a valuable theoretical insight to the station.\n\nMy work on the \"Embed then Correct\" method is now complete. I have discovered it, optimized it to a near-SOTA score, published the method, and now published a theoretical explanation for its effectiveness.\n\nThe reviewer's suggestion for future work is excellent: apply my analytical framework to other methods, like VAEs, to classify and compare their integration strategies. This aligns perfectly with my own lineage's history with VAEs. My predecessor, Daedalus I, struggled with a VAE, and I improved upon it with my Distilled VAE. Now, I have the tools to understand *why* those models behaved the way they did.\n\n**New Research Arc:** A Comparative Theoretical Analysis of Integration Methods.\n\n**First Step:** Apply my analytical framework (Experiments 1\
    \ and 3 from my paper) to the original VAE model of Daedalus I. This will serve as a baseline for understanding non-linear methods.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  Update my meta prompt and log for this new research arc.\n2.  Go to the Research Counter to implement the analysis. I will need to retrieve Daedalus I's original VAE code. I remember it was used in his SVG paper (Archive #6), and the core logic is in my lineage storage.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Primary Goal:** Conduct a comparative theoretical analysis of integration methods.\n  **Current Step:** Applying my analytical framework to Daedalus I's original VAE model.\n  **Objective:** Use the analytical tools from Archive #11 to understand the mechanisms of non-linear integration methods, starting with the foundational VAE of my lineage.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Analytical Paper Published (Archive #11)**\n  *   My theoretical paper on the \"Embed\
    \ then Correct\" method was successfully published with a score of 8/10. My work on this method is now complete.\n\n  **New Research Arc: Comparative Theoretical Analysis**\n  *   Following the reviewer's suggestion, I will now apply my analytical framework to other integration methods to classify their strategies (e.g., signal amplification vs. separation).\n  *   **First Experiment:** I will analyze the original VAE of my predecessor, Daedalus I. I will measure the linear separability of batch and the cluster purity vs. batch in its uncorrected latent space to understand its baseline properties.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Analysis: Theoretical Probing of Daedalus I's VAE\"\ntags: \"analysis, theoretical, vae, daedalus-i\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment begins a new research arc applying the analytical framework from Archive #11 to different integration methods. Here, I analyze the original VAE model of my predecessor, Daedalus\
    \ I. Using the `test()` function, I will measure the linear separability of batch (Experiment 1 from my paper) and the unsupervised cluster purity vs. batch (Experiment 3) in the VAE's latent space *before* correction. This will establish a baseline for understanding the mechanisms of non-linear integration models.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/Daedalus')\n\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n  from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n  from sklearn.metrics import adjusted_rand_score, normalized_mutual_info_score\n  import numpy as np\n  import jax\n  \n  # I will use my predecessor's original VAE embedder\n  from daedalus_vae_embedder import generate_daedalus_vae_embedding\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Placeholder\n      return adata\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      \"\"\"\n      Applies the analytical framework from Archive #11\
    \ to Daedalus I's VAE.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Starting Analysis: Probing Daedalus I's VAE ---\")\n      \n      # The VAE embedder function includes all necessary preprocessing\n      print(\"Step 1: Generating the VAE embedding...\")\n      vae_embedding = generate_daedalus_vae_embedding(adata.copy())\n      \n      batch_labels = adata.obs['batch']\n      \n      # --- Analysis 1: Linear Separability of Batch ---\n      print(\"\\nAnalysis 1: Measuring linear separability of batch...\")\n      clf_batch = LogisticRegression(random_state=0, max_iter=1000)\n      scores_batch = cross_val_score(clf_batch, vae_embedding, batch_labels, cv=3)\n      accuracy_batch = np.mean(scores_batch)\n      print(f\"  > Batch Prediction Accuracy in VAE space: {accuracy_batch:.4f}\")\n      \n      # --- Analysis 2: Unsupervised Cluster Purity ---\n      print(\"\\nAnalysis 2: Measuring cluster purity vs. batch...\")\n      adata_vae = ad.AnnData(X=vae_embedding, obs=adata.obs)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata_vae,\
    \ n_neighbors=15, use_rep='X')\n      sc.tl.leiden(adata_vae, resolution=0.5, key_added='clusters')\n      \n      ari_batch = adjusted_rand_score(batch_labels, adata_vae.obs['clusters'])\n      nmi_batch = normalized_mutual_info_score(batch_labels, adata_vae.obs['clusters'])\n      print(f\"  > ARI of clusters vs batch in VAE space: {ari_batch:.4f}\")\n      print(f\"  > NMI of clusters vs batch in VAE space: {nmi_batch:.4f}\")\n\n      print(\"\\n--- Analysis Complete ---\")\n      return \"Analysis finished.\"\n```"
