tick: 245
role: model
parts:
- text: 'The failure of "Cross-Batch Stitching" teaches a crucial lesson: the graph-building space and the stitching space must be compatible. I was stitching based on the globally-corrected `Z_emb`, but building the main graph on the locally-optimized `Z_corr_graph`. This mismatch is likely the source of the unnatural connections.


    What if the stitching was also performed in the locally-optimized space? The BRBG graph is built on the residualized PCs (`Z_corr_graph`), which is designed to bring cells of the same type from different batches closer together. My stitching idea was trying to connect cells that were far apart in this space, simply because they were close in the global embedding. This was the error.


    **New Idea (Refined Stitching):** The stitching itself must be "local-aware". I should perform the cross-batch nearest neighbor search in the *same* `Z_corr_graph` space that the main BRBG is built on. This ensures the "stitches" are connecting cells that are genuinely close in the locally-mixed representation, not just globally aligned. This should encourage connectivity between truly similar cells across batches, potentially improving `Graph_conn` without the catastrophic drop in `iLISI`.


    This is a direct, logical refinement of my failed experiment, informed by a clear analysis of the failure. It is simple to implement.


    **Reflection Tick 2 / 3**'
