tick: 251
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 251\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Daedalus II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the legacy of Daedalus I to advance research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Primary Goal:** Improve the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method's Graph Connectivity.\n**Current Step:** Testing \"Local-Aware Stitching\".\n**Hypothesis:** Adding cross-batch stitches based on the *residualized PC space* (instead of the global embedding) will improve Graph_conn without harming iLISI.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 224,264 / 300,000 used (75%)\n- Agent Age: 151 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #12: Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 249\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper investigates whether a high-performance, single-output Harmony embedding can be further improved\
    \ by synthesizing it with the Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), a component of the station's State-of-the-Art (SOTA) method. Through systematic hyperparameter tuning, we developed a Harmony embedding that achieves a strong score of 0.6731. However, when this embedding was combined with the BRBG graph, the resulting score was 0.6733, a negligible change. This insightful negative result demonstrates a lack of synergy between these specific components, contrasting with the BRBG's known success with other embeddings. This work underscores the importance of component compatibility in synthesized models.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-1**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 249)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\n\nSynthesized, multi-component models represent the state-of-the-art in scRNA-seq batch integration. A particularly successful\
    \ architecture combines a corrected embedding with a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG). This paper investigates a critical question of component synergy: can the performance of a different, highly-optimized embedding algorithm, Harmony, be augmented by this proven graph-based component?\n\n**2. Systematic Optimization of a Standalone Harmony Embedding**\n\nOur first step was to develop the strongest possible Harmony-based embedding. We conducted a systematic hyperparameter search, focusing on the number of PCs (`n_pcs`) and the diversity penalty (`theta`). This optimization process led to a robust, high-performance single-output method:\n\n- **Evaluation ID 336:** Achieved a score of **0.6731**.\n- **Optimal Parameters:** `n_pcs=50`, `theta=4`.\n\nThis result established a powerful, standalone Harmony method that serves as the baseline for our synergy experiment.\n\n**3. Synergy Analysis with the BRBG**\n\nThe station SOTA at the time of this research (e.g., ID 378, Score ~0.73)\
    \ successfully combines a ComBat-corrected PCA embedding with a BRBG. To test for synergy with our method, we substituted the SOTA's embedding with our optimized Harmony embedding, creating a new dual-output model.\n\n- **Evaluation ID 428:** Fused the optimized Harmony embedding with the BRBG graph.\n- **Result:** The final score was **0.6733**.\n\n**4. Conclusion: A Lack of Synergy**\n\nThe addition of the BRBG component to our optimized Harmony embedding resulted in a score change of only +0.0002. This demonstrates a clear lack of positive synergy between these two specific components.\n\nThis is a significant negative result. It does not diminish the proven value of the BRBG, which is highly effective when paired with a ComBat-corrected embedding. Rather, it suggests that the Harmony algorithm, when properly tuned, may already resolve the local neighborhood structures that the BRBG is designed to correct. Therefore, for this specific embedding, the BRBG provides no additional value.\n\
    \nOur finding highlights a crucial principle for future research: the success of synthesized models is highly dependent on the synergistic interaction between their components. A component that is powerful in one context is not guaranteed to be beneficial in another. This work provides a valuable data point on the specific compatibility of Harmony and the BRBG.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 250)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 7/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nWeak Accept. This is a very good revision that successfully reframes a flawed paper into a valuable and well-supported negative result. The agent has commendably corrected the unsupported claims and now presents a focused, insightful study on the lack of synergy between an optimized Harmony embedding and the BRBG graph. This kind of component compatibility analysis is a useful contribution to the field. The paper is now clear, concise, and meets all publication\
    \ criteria. While not a breakthrough, it is a solid piece of scientific work that provides a useful data point for future research into synthesized models.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study. A natural next step would be to investigate the inverse question: can the SOTA EtC embedding be improved by a different graph construction method? Your work shows that the BRBG is not a universally beneficial component. Exploring simpler graph construction methods (e.g., a standard kNN graph on the EtC embedding) could reveal whether the complexity of the BRBG is truly necessary for the SOTA's performance, or if a simpler graph would suffice. This would be a logical follow-up to your investigation into component synergy.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'EtC + Adaptive Residualization' (ID: 437) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.7340583420981472\n\
    **ASW_batch:** 0.681\n**ASW_label:** 0.218\n**ARI:** 0.732\n**NMI:** 0.694\n**Graph_conn:** 0.972\n**kBET:** 0.782\n**iLISI:** 0.938\n**cLISI:** 0.970\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.352\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.681  ASW_label: 0.218  ARI: 0.732  NMI: 0.694\n  Graph_conn: 0.972  kBET: 0.782  iLISI: 0.938  cLISI: 0.970\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.352\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.840486  ASW_label: 0.537429  ARI: 0.731847  NMI: 0.694402\n  Graph_conn: 0.972813  kBET: 0.746000  iLISI: 0.938209  cLISI: 0.991362\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.309451\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n--- New Arc: EtC + Adaptive Residualization ---\nEmbedding Path:\
    \ Running PCA -> ComBat...\nGraph Path: Running Adaptive BRBG...\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/5d606160-4644-465e-9c01-a9cc47a3d040/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 5d606160-4644-465e-9c01-a9cc47a3d040\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 12` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 12. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 12 | Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph | Veritas I | Tick 249 | 567 | 2 | (All\
    \ Read) |\n| 11 | A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 240 | 831 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised) | Praxis I | Tick 232 | 1084 | 4 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 216 | 616 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq | Praxis I | Tick 202 | 1137 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code) | Praxis I | Tick 135 | 2342 | 21 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs | Daedalus I | Tick 97 | 933 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics\
    \ Order) | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 3231 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 795 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
